  "activity": {
    "id": "act_vectors_projectile",
    "name": "From Vectors to Projectile Motion",
    "description": "A linear AR lab for algebra-based General Physics I. Students first explore vectors as magnitude-and-direction quantities, add them tip-to-tail, and decompose them into components, then apply these tools to two-dimensional projectile motion \u2014 observing the independence of horizontal and vertical motion and discovering the 45-degree maximum-range result.",
    "workplace": "wp_vectors_projectile",
    "start": "step_intro_vectors",
    "actions": [
      {
        "id": "step_intro_vectors",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "1. What Is a Vector?",
          "description": "A single arrow floats at the origin. Grab its head and drag it in 3D; watch the magnitude and direction labels update in real time. A vector has both a size (magnitude) and a direction, unlike a scalar such as temperature or mass. Rotate your view to confirm the arrow itself does not change \u2014 only your viewpoint. Click Next when ready."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "thing_reference_axes",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_reference_axes",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_vector_arrow",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_single_vector_anim",
              "poi": "default",
              "state": "idle_draggable"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_vector_workspace",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "lbl_magnitude_direction",
              "poi": "poi_label_slot",
              "text": "Magnitude: 27.4 | Direction: 33\u00b0 x, 61\u00b0 y, 74\u00b0 z"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_vector_workspace",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "narr_intro_vectors",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "thing_vector_arrow",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_single_vector_anim"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_vector_workspace",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "lbl_magnitude_direction"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_vector_workspace",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "narr_intro_vectors"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_addition"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_addition",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "2. Adding Vectors Tip-to-Tail",
          "description": "Two vectors appear \u2014 blue starts at the origin, green starts at the head of blue (tip-to-tail). The red resultant runs from the tail of blue to the head of green. Reorient either vector and watch the resultant update. Swap the order of blue and green and confirm the resultant lands in the same place: order does not matter. Click Next to continue."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "thing_vector_arrow",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_vector_addition_anim",
              "poi": "default",
              "state": "tip_to_tail"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_vector_workspace",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "lbl_resultant",
              "poi": "poi_label_slot",
              "text": "Resultant R = A + B = B + A"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_vector_workspace",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "narr_addition",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "thing_vector_arrow",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_vector_addition_anim"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_vector_workspace",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "lbl_resultant"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_vector_workspace",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "narr_addition"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_decomposition"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_decomposition",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "3. Decomposing a Vector into Components",
          "description": "One vector appears, then three color-coded component arrows emerge from the origin \u2014 x, then y (from the tip of x), then z (from the tip of y) \u2014 laid tip-to-tail to reproduce the original. Resize each component and watch the original update. Decomposition is just addition in reverse. In 2D the components and magnitude form a right triangle (Pythagorean theorem). Click Next to move to projectile motion."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "thing_vector_arrow",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_decomposition_anim",
              "poi": "default",
              "state": "xyz_components"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_vector_workspace",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "lbl_components",
              "poi": "poi_label_slot",
              "text": "Vx + Vy + Vz reconstruct V"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_vector_workspace",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "img_pythagorean_triangle",
              "poi": "poi_label_slot"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_vector_workspace",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "narr_decomposition",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "thing_vector_arrow",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_decomposition_anim"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_vector_workspace",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "lbl_components"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_vector_workspace",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "img_pythagorean_triangle"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_vector_workspace",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "narr_decomposition"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_reference_axes",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_reference_axes"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_projectile_intro"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_projectile_intro",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "4. Setting Up the Projectile",
          "description": "The 3D workspace gives way to a 2D vertical plane with labeled horizontal and vertical axes. Because gravity acts only downward and no horizontal force acts, the whole motion stays in this plane \u2014 a genuinely 2D problem. A ball rests on the ground line at left, with a velocity arrow pointing up and to the right. Point your device at the plane marker to load the scene, or click Next."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "place_vertical_plane",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_projectile_setup_anim",
              "poi": "default",
              "state": "ball_at_rest"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_projectile_ball",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_projectile_setup_anim",
              "poi": "poi_ball_top",
              "state": "velocity_vector_shown"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_vertical_plane",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "lbl_plane_axes",
              "poi": "poi_plane_label",
              "text": "Horizontal (x) & Vertical (y) axes"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_vertical_plane",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "narr_projectile_intro",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "thing_projectile_ball",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_projectile_setup_anim"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_vertical_plane",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "narr_projectile_intro"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_flight_demo"
          },
          {
            "mode": "detect",
            "id": "det_plane_marker"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_flight_demo",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "5. Independent Motions in Flight",
          "description": "The velocity is decomposed into a constant horizontal component (right) and a changing vertical component (up). Launch the ball: the horizontal arrow stays fixed while the vertical arrow shrinks on ascent, reaches zero at the peak, then grows downward on descent. The parabolic trail appears behind the ball. Switch to overhead view to see the parabola flatten to a line \u2014 horizontal and vertical motions are independent. Click Next."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "thing_projectile_ball",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_projectile_flight_anim",
              "poi": "poi_ball_top",
              "state": "component_flight"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_vertical_plane",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "lbl_peak_v_zero",
              "poi": "poi_plane_label",
              "text": "At max height: vertical velocity = 0"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_vertical_plane",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "narr_independence",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "thing_projectile_ball",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_projectile_flight_anim"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_vertical_plane",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "lbl_peak_v_zero"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_vertical_plane",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "narr_independence"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_max_range"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_max_range",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "6. Discovering Maximum Range",
          "description": "Set the launch angle (0\u201390\u00b0) and speed, then launch. The range is displayed and recorded in a history panel so you can compare attempts. Try several angles at a fixed speed. You will find that range is maximized at 45\u00b0, and that angles symmetric about 45\u00b0 (e.g., 30\u00b0 & 60\u00b0) give the same range \u2014 because range is greatest when the horizontal and vertical velocity components are equal. Click Finish when done."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "thing_projectile_ball",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_range_explorer_anim",
              "poi": "poi_ball_top",
              "state": "adjustable_launch"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_target",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pred_range_explorer_anim",
              "poi": "default",
              "state": "range_marker"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_vertical_plane",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "lbl_range_result",
              "poi": "poi_plane_label",
              "text": "Max range at 45\u00b0: Vx = Vy"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_vertical_plane",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "narr_max_range",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": true,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_max_range"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
